<BaseLayout title="Reference" description="A selective map of authoritative sources, benchmarks, and stewardship guidance." current="reference">
  <h1>Reference</h1>
  <p class="lede">Use each source for its distinct role; its access route, version, and scientific boundary remain part of the citation.</p>

  <h2>Continuous sources</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/electronic-structure/ED0FF348536BFFE8899627C8F78FEE6A">Electronic Structure: Basic Theory and Practical Methods</a></dt>
    <dd>Richard M. Martin, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2020, DOI 10.1017/9781108555586; publisher metadata and preview are public, while full access depends on purchase or institutional entitlement and no reuse licence is implied. Use it for a continuous theory-to-method argument, then separate later developments from the source.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://www.materialscloud.org/learn">Materials Cloud Learn</a></dt>
    <dd>A rolling collection of recorded computational-materials schools. Use a dated school for a coherent implementation route; its software version, exercise files, access terms, and licence are item-specific.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://education.molssi.org/">MolSSI Education</a></dt>
    <dd>An openly accessible, rolling collection of molecular-science programming and modelling lessons. Use it for software and molecular-method foundations; each lesson states its own repository, licence, date, and scope.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Verification collections</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><a href="https://sssp.materialscloud.org/">SSSP</a></dt>
    <dd>A versioned Materials Cloud verification collection for pseudopotential precision and efficiency. Cite the exact release and component; each pseudopotential retains its original licence, and covered benchmarks do not replace system- and observable-specific validation.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://www.pseudo-dojo.org/">PseudoDojo</a></dt>
    <dd>A versioned pseudopotential generation and grading project with public downloads. Use a named dataset and its documented tests, and check that dataset’s reuse terms; functional, relativistic treatment, transferability, and target observable remain separate choices.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Stewardship</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><a href="https://www.go-fair.org/fair-principles/">FAIR Principles</a></dt>
    <dd>Community guidance for making digital objects findable, accessible, interoperable, and reusable; the public text is available under CC BY 4.0. Use it to design metadata and access, not as evidence that data are correct.</dd>
    <dt><a href="https://archive.materialscloud.org/">Materials Cloud Archive</a></dt>
    <dd>A repository of dated, DOI-addressable materials-data records. Use the specific record’s version, files, metadata, and licence; platform presence does not establish completeness or suitability for a later claim.</dd>
  </dl>
</BaseLayout>
